The $z$ component of the <cross product> is $2a-2$. For the result to lie in the $x$-$y$ <plane>, it must vanish, giving $a=1$. The <dot product> condition is then $2a+2-3b=0$, so
$$
\boxed{a=1,\qquad b=\frac43}.
$$

= 2
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=2}

Using the exponential definition of the <hyperbolic cosine> and <Euler's formula>,
$$
\cosh(\alpha+i\beta)=\cosh\alpha\cos\beta+i\sinh\alpha\sin\beta.
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Re}=\cosh\alpha\cos\beta,\qquad
\operatorname{Im}=\sinh\alpha\sin\beta}.
$$

= 3
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=3}

Multiply the standard <Taylor series> through quadratic order:
$$
e^x\log(1+x)=\left(1+x+\frac{x^2}{2}+\cdots\right)
\left(x-\frac{x^2}{2}+\cdots\right).
$$
The linear term is $x$ and the quadratic coefficient is $1-1/2$, hence
$$
\boxed{f(x)=x+\frac{x^2}{2}+O(x^3)}.
$$

= 4
{parent=Paper 2}
