= Solution
{parent=4}

The <characteristic equation> of the homogeneous equation is $(r-1)(r-3)=0$. Thus the <complementary function> is
$$
\boxed{y_c=Ae^x+Be^{3x}}.
$$
A linear trial $y_p=ax+b$ gives $3ax+(3b-4a)=3x$, so $a=1$, $b=4/3$. One <particular integral> is therefore $\boxed{y_p=x+4/3}$.

= 5
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=5}

Take the Cartesian <partial derivatives> component by component. The <divergence> is
$$
\boxed{\nabla\cdot\mathbf F=-e^{-x}\cos z-e^{-y}\sin z}.
$$
The constant third component has zero <derivatives>. The <curl> consequently has only two nonzero components:
$$
\boxed{\nabla\times\mathbf F=(-e^{-y}\cos z,-e^{-x}\sin z,0)}.
$$

= 6
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=6}

Set $\xi=x-ct$. The <chain rule> gives $u_t=-cf'(\xi)$ and $u_x=f'(\xi)$, so $u_t+cu_x=0$ for any differentiable $f$. The initial profile fixes $f(\xi)=\cos\xi$, giving
$$
\boxed{u(x,t)=\cos(x-ct)}.
$$
This is the translated initial profile, as described by the <transport equation>.

