= Solution
{parent=i}

A real square <orthogonal matrix> $Q$ satisfies
$$
\boxed{Q^TQ=I},
$$
equivalently $Q^{-1}=Q^T$. Its columns, and also its rows, form an <orthonormal basis>. Consequently it preserves <dot products> and <Euclidean norms>.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

To invert the <matrix>, row-reduce the augmented <matrix> $(M\mid I)$. Subtracting row one from rows two and three gives left rows $(0,-4,1)$ and $(0,0,2)$, so the pivots are nonzero and back-substitution is possible. The resulting <matrix inverse> is
$$
\boxed{M^{-1}=\begin{pmatrix}
3/8&1/4&3/8\\1/8&-1/4&1/8\\-1/2&0&1/2
\end{pmatrix}}.
$$
Multiplying by $M$ verifies the identity <matrix>. This inverse is not $M^T$; indeed
$$
M^TM=\begin{pmatrix}3&-1&0\\-1&11&0\\0&0&2\end{pmatrix}\ne I.
$$
Thus **$M$ is not <orthogonal>**.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Use the order-reversal rule for the <transpose> of a product:
$$
(AB)^T(AB)=B^TA^TAB=B^TIB=B^TB=I.
$$
Therefore **the product of two <orthogonal> <matrices> is <orthogonal>**. The argument does not require $A$ and $B$ to commute.

