= Solution
{parent=i}

Collect the coefficients of the unknowns into the <matrix> and the constants into a column. The requested <linear system> is
$$
\boxed{\begin{pmatrix}1&1&1\\2&2&a-1\\a&1&3\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}x\\y\\z\end{pmatrix}
=\begin{pmatrix}3\\4\\5\end{pmatrix}}.
$$
The unknown coordinate $y$ here is a scalar component; the right-hand column is the <vector> denoted by the question's matrix-form notation.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

At $a=2$, eliminate the first column by $R_2\leftarrow R_2-2R_1$ and $R_3\leftarrow R_3-2R_1$ in the augmented <matrix>. The last two rows give $-z=-2$ and $-y+z=-1$. Hence $z=2$, $y=3$, and the first row then gives $x=-2$. Thus
$$
\boxed{(x,y,z)=(-2,3,2)}.
$$
The coefficient <determinant> at this parameter is nonzero, so this solution is unique.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The same elimination at general $a$ gives the rows
$$
\begin{pmatrix}1&1&1&|&3\\0&0&a-3&|&-2\\
0&1-a&3-a&|&5-3a\end{pmatrix}.
$$
The coefficient <determinant> is $(a-3)(a-1)$. Only $a=1$ or $a=3$ can therefore fail to have a unique solution. At $a=3$, the second row reads $0=-2$, so **there is no solution**. At $a=1$, the last two rows are proportional and both specify $z=1$, leaving one free unknown; hence **there are infinitely many solutions**. Every other real $a$ gives a unique solution.

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

At $a=1$, the reduced system gives $z=1$ and $x+y=2$. Thus all solutions are
$$
\boxed{(x,y,z)=(2-s,s,1),\qquad s\in\mathbb R}.
$$
Geometrically the <solution set> is an affine straight line, and substitution verifies all three original equations.

