The hyperbolic identity $\cosh^2x-\sinh^2x=1$ and $\cosh x+\sinh x=e^x$ reduce the integrand to $1+e^x$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\int(\cosh^2x+\cosh x-\sinh^2x+\sinh x)\,dx=x+e^x+C}.
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Let $D=3x^2+2x+2$. Splitting the numerator into a <derivative> term and a constant gives $x-1=D'/6-4/3$. Thus
$$
\int\frac{x-1}{D}\,dx=\frac16\log D-\frac43\int\frac{dx}{D}.
$$
Complete the square as $D=((3x+1)^2+5)/3$ and substitute $u=3x+1$. The remaining <integral> is $\int du/(u^2+5)=\arctan(u/\sqrt5)/\sqrt5$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\frac16\log(3x^2+2x+2)
-\frac4{3\sqrt5}\arctan\frac{3x+1}{\sqrt5}+C}.
$$
The quadratic denominator is positive for all real $x$, so no logarithmic absolute value or real-domain exclusion is needed.

= b
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The argument $3x^3+2x$ is odd, so its squared <sine> is even. Replacing $x$ by $-x$ interchanges the numerator and denominator inside the <logarithm>, changing that factor's sign. Thus the full integrand is an <odd function>. It is continuous on the symmetric interval because $|x|\leq1/\pi<1$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\int_{-1/\pi}^{1/\pi}\sin^2(3x^3+2x)
\log\frac{1-x^5}{1+x^5}\,dx=0}.
$$

= c
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <fundamental theorem of calculus> states that integrating a continuous <function> from a fixed lower endpoint produces an <antiderivative>. More precisely, if $f$ is continuous at the interior point $x$, then
$$
\boxed{\frac{d}{dx}\int_a^x f(y)\,dy=f(x)}.
$$
Indeed, the difference quotient is the average of $f$ over the short interval from $x$ to $x+h$, which tends to $f(x)$ by <continuity>. The accompanying evaluation rule is $\int_a^b f=F(b)-F(a)$ whenever $F'=f$ on the interval.

= d
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The sum is finite, so <differentiation> passes through it without a convergence issue. Each lower limit $n$ is constant with respect to $x$, and the <fundamental theorem of calculus> gives the same upper-end integrand. Hence the <derivative> is
$$
\sin(x^2+x^6)\sum_{n=0}^N\binom Nn.
$$
The <binomial theorem> at $1+1$ gives the sum $2^N$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\frac{d}{dx}\left[\sum_{n=0}^N\binom Nn
\int_n^x\sin(y^2+y^6)\,dy\right]=2^N\sin(x^2+x^6)}.
$$

= 14
