= Solution
{parent=a}

Writing the two coefficients as $M,N$, their mixed <derivatives> satisfy $M_y=2xy=N_x$, so the smooth form is exact on $\mathbb R^2$. Integrating the first coefficient with respect to $x$ gives $f=2x^2+x^2y^2/2+h(y)$. Matching $f_y=y+x^2y$ gives $h'(y)=y$. One potential is therefore
$$
\boxed{f(x,y)=2x^2+\frac12(1+x^2)y^2}.
$$
The <integral curves> of $df=0$ are its <level curves>. Absorbing a factor of two into the constant gives
$$
4x^2+(1+x^2)y^2=C,\qquad
\boxed{y=\pm\sqrt{\frac{C-4x^2}{1+x^2}}}
$$
on real graph branches. The initial condition fixes $C=12$ and selects the positive branch:
$$
\boxed{y(x)=2\sqrt{\frac{3-x^2}{1+x^2}},\qquad-\sqrt3<x<\sqrt3}.
$$
This is the maximal smooth graph interval containing $x=1$; at either endpoint the <level curve> has a vertical tangent, so $y'$ cannot remain finite.

= b
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Exactness after multiplication requires $\partial_y(\mu M)=\partial_x(\mu N)$. Since $\mu$ depends only on $x$,
$$
\mu M_y=\mu' N+\mu N_x.
$$
For $\mu=\exp(\int f(x)\,dx)$, the ratio $\mu'/\mu$ is $f(x)$, so
$$
\boxed{f(x)=\frac{M_y-N_x}{N}},\qquad
\boxed{\mu(x)=\exp\int\frac{M_y-N_x}{N}\,dx}.
$$
The assumed existence means this ratio is a <function> only of $x$ wherever the division is valid. For a factor depending only on $y$, the corresponding equation is $\psi'M+\psi M_y=\psi N_x$, giving
$$
\boxed{\psi(y)=\exp\int\frac{N_x-M_y}{M}\,dy}.
$$
These are the <integrating factor for a differential one-form> formulas. Nonzero constant multiples are equivalent. Exactness follows locally from closedness, and globally on a suitable <simply connected domain>.

= c
{parent=14}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Here $M_y-N_x=(6xy+2)-(4xy+1)=2xy+1$, whereas $N=x(2xy+1)$. Thus the ratio in (b) is $1/x$, and one may choose the nonzero <integrating factor> $\boxed{\mu(x)=x}$ on either half-line. A negative constant multiple on a half-line is harmless.

The multiplied form is
$$
(3x^2y^2+2xy)\,dx+(2x^3y+x^2)\,dy
=d(x^3y^2+x^2y).
$$
Hence $x^3y^2+x^2y=C$, a quadratic equation for $y$. Solving it gives
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\frac{-1\pm\sqrt{1+4C/x}}{2x}},\qquad x\ne0.
$$
Choose a real branch with positive discriminant to obtain a differentiable graph; a zero discriminant produces a vertical tangent. Although the ratio derivation initially cancels $2xy+1$, the displayed exact differential verifies the multiplying factor directly wherever $x\ne0$. At $C=0$ the branches are $y=0$ and $y=-1/x$. Only $y=0$ has a finite smooth extension across $x=0$, where the original equation requires $y=0$.

= 15
{parent=Paper 2}
