= Solution
{parent=b}

The first field has vanishing <curl> and is the <gradient> of a <monomial>:
$$
\boxed{\nabla\times\mathbf F_1=\mathbf0,\qquad
\phi_1=x^3y^2z+C,\qquad\mathbf F_1=\nabla\phi_1}.
$$
For the second field, direct component <differentiation> gives
$$
\boxed{\nabla\times\mathbf F_2=
(-2x^3y,\,3x^2z(2y-z),\,3x^2z(2y-z))}.
$$
It is not identically zero. Thus **$\mathbf F_1$ is conservative and $\mathbf F_2$ is not** on $\mathbb R^3$. The conditional path-integral requests therefore apply to $\mathbf F_1$.

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Parametrize the straight path by $\mathbf r(t)=(t,t,2t)$ for $0\leq t\leq1$. The conservative field along it is $(6t^5,4t^5,t^5)$ and the tangent is $(1,1,2)$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\int_A^B\mathbf F_1\cdot d\mathbf r=\int_0^1 12t^5\,dt=2}.
$$
The associated potential has $\phi_1(B)-\phi_1(A)=2$, in agreement with the <fundamental theorem for line integrals>.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For $\mathbf r(t)=(t,t,2t^3)$, the conservative field is $(6t^7,4t^7,t^5)$ and the tangent is $(1,1,6t^2)$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\int_A^B\mathbf F_1\cdot d\mathbf r=\int_0^1 16t^7\,dt=2}.
$$
The two different paths give the same value because their <line integrals> depend only on the potential difference $\phi_1(B)-\phi_1(A)=2$. There is no <scalar potential> for $\mathbf F_2$ to which the conditional request could be applied.

