{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <gradient> is $\nabla f=(2x,z,y)$, so its value at the point is $(2,-3,1)$. Since the specified direction is already a <unit vector>, the <directional derivative> is its <dot product> with that <gradient>:
$$
\boxed{D_{\hat{\mathbf u}}f=\frac{(2,-3,1)\cdot(2,1,2)}3
=\frac{4-3+2}{3}=1}.
$$

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For a unit direction $\mathbf v$, the <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality> gives $\nabla f\cdot\mathbf v\leq|\nabla f|$, with equality in the <gradient> direction. Its norm here is $\sqrt{4+9+1}=\sqrt{14}$, so the direction of fastest increase is
$$
\boxed{\mathbf v_{\max}=\frac{(2,-3,1)}{\sqrt{14}}}.
$$
The maximal <directional derivative> itself is $\sqrt{14}$.

= 18
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Differentiating gives
$$
y'=e^{\sin^2x}\sin2x,\qquad
y''=e^{\sin^2x}\left(\sin^22x+2\cos2x\right).
$$
The exponential never vanishes, so all stationary points have $x=k\pi/2$. At $x=k\pi$, $y=1$ and $y''=2>0$; at $x=\pi/2+k\pi$, $y=e$ and $y''=-2e<0$. Thus
$$
\boxed{(k\pi,1)\text{ are minima},\qquad
(\pi/2+k\pi,e)\text{ are maxima},\quad k\in\mathbb Z}.
$$
These are <global minima> and maxima because $0\leq\sin^2x\leq1$. None of the stationary points is an <inflection point>.

= b
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The graph is even and periodic with period $\pi$, bounded between $1$ and $e$. It rises from each minimum at $k\pi$ to a maximum halfway to the next minimum, then falls back. It has horizontal tangents at all these extrema and repeats smoothly.

\Image[paper-2-exponential-sine-square.png]
{title=The periodic graph of exp(sin squared x), with all types of stationary point marked}

= c
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For the composite <square root>, $y>1$ throughout the restricted $x$ interval. Differentiate with respect to $y$:
$$
\boxed{\frac{dz}{dy}=\frac1{2y\sqrt{\log y}}},\qquad
\boxed{\frac{d^2z}{dy^2}=-\frac{2\log y+1}{4y^2(\log y)^{3/2}}}.
$$
The second expression comes from differentiating both $y^{-1}$ and $(\log y)^{-1/2}$. The endpoints where $y=1$ are excluded here.

= d
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

On $0<x<\pi$, $\sqrt{\log y}=\sqrt{\sin^2x}=\sin x>0$. The <chain rule> gives
$$
\frac{dz}{dx}=\frac{dz}{dy}\frac{dy}{dx}
=\frac{1}{2y\sin x}\,y\sin2x.
$$
Using $\sin2x=2\sin x\cos x$ yields
$$
\boxed{\frac{dz}{dx}=\cos x}.
$$

= e
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

The <second derivative chain rule> is
$$
\frac{d^2z}{dx^2}=\frac{d^2z}{dy^2}(y')^2+\frac{dz}{dy}y''.
$$
Put $s=\sin x>0$ and $c_0=\cos x$. Substituting the preceding results and $y'=2ysc_0$ gives
$$
\frac{d^2z}{dx^2}
=-\frac{(2s^2+1)c_0^2}{s}
+2sc_0^2+\frac{c_0^2-s^2}{s}=-s.
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{\frac{d^2z}{dx^2}=-\sin x}.
$$

= f
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

Substitution first makes the square-root branch transparent:
$$
z=\sqrt{\log(e^{\sin^2x})}=\sqrt{\sin^2x}=|\sin x|.
$$
On the given interval this is exactly $\sin x$, so direct <differentiation> gives
$$
\boxed{z''=-\sin x},
$$
in agreement with the chain-rule calculation. Outside this interval one must retain the absolute value; there are cusps at integer multiples of $\pi$.

= 19
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=19}

For a short string element of length $\Delta x$, the small-slope approximation makes the upward components of <tension> at its two ends $T y_x(x+\Delta x,t)$ and $-Ty_x(x,t)$. Its <mass> is $\rho\Delta x$. <Newton's second law> gives
$$
\rho\Delta x\,y_{tt}=T\left[y_x(x+\Delta x,t)-y_x(x,t)\right].
$$
Divide by $\Delta x$ and take the limit to obtain the <wave equation on a string>,
$$
\boxed{y_{tt}=c^2y_{xx},\qquad c=\sqrt{\frac T\rho}}.
$$
Here $\rho$ is <linear mass density>, and the derivation assumes small transverse slopes, uniform <tension>, and a flexible string.

At the right endpoint the string pulls the ring toward the interior. Its vertical force is therefore $-Ty_x(L,t)$ to first order; the frictionless wire supplies no vertical force. The ring obeys
$$
\boxed{M y_{tt}(L,t)=-Ty_x(L,t)}.
$$
This is the dynamic endpoint law for a <string with an endpoint mass>.

For a separated oscillatory solution, write $y=X(x)q(t)$ with $q''=-\omega^2q$ and $X''+(\omega/c)^2X=0$. The fixed left end removes the spatial <cosine>, so the nonzero <normal modes> have
$$
y_n(x,t)=\left[A_n\cos(\omega_nt)+B_n\sin(\omega_nt)\right]
\sin\frac{\omega_nx}{c}.
$$
The endpoint law gives $M\omega_n^2\sin(\omega_nL/c)=T(\omega_n/c)\cos(\omega_nL/c)$. Since $\sin(\omega_nL/c)=0$ would also force the <cosine> to vanish, it can be divided out for a nonzero <mode>. Using $T=\rho c^2$ gives
$$
\boxed{\cot\frac{\omega_nL}{c}=\frac{M\omega_n}{\rho c}}.
$$
For physical $M\geq0$, these give all positive modal frequencies. There is no nonzero static linear <mode>: the left value and right slope conditions would force it to vanish. Positivity also follows by <integration by parts> from
$$
\omega^2\left(\rho\int_0^L X^2\,dx+MX(L)^2\right)
=T\int_0^L(X')^2\,dx.
$$

For $M=0$, the right endpoint is free, with the <Neumann boundary condition> $y_x(L,t)=0$. At $L=\pi$, the allowed wave numbers are $k_n=n+1/2$, $n\geq0$, and $\omega_n=ck_n$. Zero initial displacement removes every cosine-in-time coefficient. The initial velocity contains only the modes $k=1/2$ and $k=5/2$, each with coefficient one. Dividing those velocity coefficients by their angular frequencies gives
$$
\boxed{y(x,t)=\frac2c\sin\frac{ct}{2}\sin\frac x2
+\frac{2}{5c}\sin\frac{5ct}{2}\sin\frac{5x}{2}}.
$$
At $x=0$ each term vanishes, at $x=\pi$ each spatial <derivative> vanishes, and at $t=0$ the displacement and its time <derivative> reproduce the two <initial conditions>.

= 20
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Assume real square-integrable functions on the same integration domain. Put $A=\int f^2$, $B=\int g^2$, and $C=\int fg$. If $B=0$, then $g=0$ almost everywhere, so $C=0$ and the claim follows. If $B>0$, nonnegativity of a square gives
$$
0\leq\int\left(f-\frac CBg\right)^2\,dx
=A-\frac{C^2}{B}.
$$
Multiplying by $B$ proves the <integral> form of the <Cauchy-Schwarz inequality>:
$$
\boxed{\left(\int f^2\,dx\right)\left(\int g^2\,dx\right)
\geq\left(\int fg\,dx\right)^2}.
$$
For nonzero functions, equality holds exactly when they are proportional almost everywhere. The zero-function cases also give equality.

= b
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The integrand and both limits depend on $x$, so use the <Leibniz integral rule>. The upper and lower endpoint contributions and the interior <derivative> give
$$
F'(x)=2x\sin(x^3)-3\sin(3x^2)+\int_{3x}^{x^2}t\cos(xt)\,dt.
$$
For $x\ne0$, an <antiderivative> in $t$ is $t\sin(xt)/x+\cos(xt)/x^2$. Evaluating it and collecting terms gives
$$
\boxed{F'(x)=3x\sin(x^3)-6\sin(3x^2)
+\frac{\cos(x^3)-\cos(3x^2)}{x^2}},\qquad x\ne0.
$$
At $x=0$ the original Leibniz expression has zero endpoint terms and an <integral> over a zero-length interval, giving $\boxed{F'(0)=0}$. The displayed quotient has a removable singularity and the same limiting value.

= c
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The parameter <derivative> of the integrand is $\cos(a\log x)$, whose absolute value is bounded by one on $0<x<1$. This integrable bound justifies <differentiation under the integral sign>, giving
$$
I'(a)=\int_0^1\cos(a\log x)\,dx.
$$
The original <integral> also exists: $|\sin(a\log x)/\log x|\leq|a|$, and its apparent singularity at $x=1$ is removable. Set $x=e^{-t}$ to evaluate the <derivative>:
$$
I'(a)=\int_0^\infty e^{-t}\cos(at)\,dt
=\operatorname{Re}\int_0^\infty e^{-(1-ia)t}\,dt
=\operatorname{Re}\frac1{1-ia}.
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{I'(a)=\frac1{1+a^2}}.
$$
Since $I(0)=0$, integration in the parameter fixes the constant and gives the <logarithmic sine integral> identity
$$
\boxed{I(a)=\arctan a,\qquad I(1)=\frac\pi4}.
$$
